====================================================================
REAL-DATA DIFFERENTIAL (attack surface 1) -- IDENTITY HOLDS EVERYWHERE
====================================================================
Loaded the real ledger (1275 txns post-dedup/classify) through the actual
production functions in both trees via httptest/direct calls (not the
server binary). For every differing figure the delta exactly equals
2x(sum of positive-amount outflow rows in that bucket), confirming the
abs-vs-signed algebra end to end on real data, e.g.:

  Major-expense drilldown (via HTTP handler, both trees):
    Amazon                              old=19449.96  new=18753.24  delta=696.72 = 2*348.36
    Home improvement -- hardware stores  old=7570.04   new=5368.12  delta=2201.92 = 2*1100.96
    Disputed charges (PB Digital/Cybernet) old=981.49  new=752.49   delta=229.00 = 2*114.50
    vacation FL #1                       old=1275.50   new=875.94   delta=399.56 = 2*199.78
    "Other" rollup                       old=46345.97  new=45187.21 delta=1158.76 (sum of member deltas)
  Merchant chart (buildMerchantsChartData, direct call):
    Travel & vacations   old=1096.05  new=-159.95  delta=1256.00 = 2*628.00  (renders NEGATIVE bar, honest)
    Ignore               old=3        new=-3       delta=6.00   = 2*3.00
  Cumulative cash-flow (buildCumulativeChartData, direct call, 475 days):
    old final=-47635.68  new final=-41721.76  delta=-5913.92 = -2*2956.96 (sum of all positive-amount
    outflow rows in the ledger); negative-amount Income rows = 0 in this ledger so that leg of the
    identity contributes 0, consistent.
  MajorExpenseTrends (insights.MajorExpenseTrends, direct call, March 2025 window
  -- the real cruise-refund cluster, 13 raw CC rows / 8 post-dedup):
    Travel & vacations   old=628 (abs)  new=-628 (signed)  delta=1256 = 2*628 (matches merchant posSum exactly)

Enumerated all 33 real positive-amount Outflow-typed rows in the ledger
(attack surface 4). Every one is plausibly a genuine refund/credit given its
description: Amazon/Home Depot/Fitbit/Ui.com/Shop Hampton returns, "ATM Fee
Rebate", "ADJ DISPUTE-xxxxx" chargeback credits, "Cruises" refunds (all 13
raw rows negative in the source CC file, flipped positive by the documented
credit-card sign-convention flip in loader.go:536-551 -- exactly the
"belongs in the loader" mechanism ruling CB3-2026-09-02a describes), and a
"Youtube Member" +1.99 that is the same-day reversal of a -1.99 charge 15
days earlier in the same checking-account file (a real refund, not an
unsigned export). Traced the credit-card-convention detector
(usesCreditCardSignConvention, loader.go:583-608): it runs before the
classifier and is exactly the "unsigned bank export" escape hatch the
ruling says belongs in the loader, not per-chart. I could not construct a
real CSV-through-dataloader counterexample where a positive-amount Outflow
row is NOT a refund -- ruling CB3-2026-09-02a's premise holds against this
real dataset. (I flag as a narrower, unresolved theoretical gap, not a
counterexample: a mixed-convention bank file with ANY bank-only signal --
e.g. one paycheck -- disqualifies the WHOLE file from the CC-convention
flip per hasBankOnlySignal, so a single mis-signed row inside an otherwise-
bank-convention file colliding with a NeverIncomeKeywords term like
"subscription"/"fee"/"debit" would reach the classifier unflipped and stay
positive without being a refund. No such row exists in this real ledger;
this is a pre-existing classifier/loader property, not something CB3
touched, and belongs on the backlog, not this verdict.)

