Pre-existing (OUT OF SCOPE, not part of this FAIL): bucketMajorExpenses'
`if total > 0` filter (handlers.go ~1300, unchanged by CB3's diff, confirmed
via python3 difflib against master) silently drops any major-expense group
whose signed net is <= 0 from the ENTIRE donut/list/drilldown surface --
not just the modal. On the real ledger's default full-history window,
"Travel & vacations" (net -159.95, the group the spec's own motivating
example is about) is invisible on this surface in BOTH master and fix: it
never appears as a wedge, never in "Other", never reachable by name via
handleMajorExpenseDrilldown's `default` case. The spec's framing ("today
the Travel group's modal DISAGREES with its own list row") does not hold
against this real dataset's default view -- there is no list row to
disagree with, because bucketMajorExpenses hides it first. (It does show
correctly, net-negative, on the merchant chart, and does show correctly
signed for narrower windows like March 2025 above, where its net happens to
be reachable in the top-10-by-|change| trend list.) This is pre-existing
code the worker did not touch; flagging for the backlog, not failing on it.

====================================================================
IN-SCOPE DEFECT (grounds for FAIL): CB3-D's signed previous can now be
negative, and MajorExpenseTrends' own inline percent-change/direction
classifier was never guarded for it -- produces self-contradictory output,
reproduced on real production data
====================================================================
internal/services/insights/trends.go, MajorExpenseTrends (the exact
function CB3-D modified), lines ~68-86 (previous==0 branch and the general
`changePercent = ((current-previous)/previous)*100` branch) were NOT part
of CB3-D's diff, but they are the direct DOWNSTREAM CONSUMER the spec's own
text commands the worker to trace: "any division, threshold, or projection
consuming these that misbehaves on negatives ... is a STOP-and-report
unless a trivial guard preserves current behavior." Before CB3-D, `current`
and `previous` were always >= 0 (abs-based), so this arithmetic could never
divide by a negative or emit a negative-from-zero case; CB3-D's signing
makes both newly capable of going negative, and this code has no |previous|
guard analogous to the one CB3-E documents and pins for metrics.PercentChange
in the SAME task.

Two concrete, real-data-reproduced failure modes:

1. previous == 0, current < 0 (a refund-dominant period following a
   zero-baseline period): the code hard-codes changePercent=100,
   direction="up" for ANY nonzero current when previous is 0 -- correct
   pre-CB3 (current could only be positive), WRONG post-CB3-D. Reproduced
   on the worker's OWN committed fixture
   (TestAnalyzeMajorExpenseTrends_RefundDominantPeriodSignedNet,
   trends_test.go:654): that test asserts CurrentAmount == -500 but never
   checks Direction/ChangePercent. Actual values, confirmed by direct call:
     current=-500 previous=0 change_amount=-500 change_percent=100 direction="up"
   i.e. a $500 net REFUND is reported as "spending up 100%".

2. previous < 0 (a normal period immediately following a refund-dominant
   one): `(current-previous)/previous` divides by a negative number,
   flipping the sign relative to change_amount. Reproduced on REAL
   production ledger data, no synthetic fixture needed -- April 2025,
   direct call to MajorExpenseTrends(active, defs, pins, Apr1, Apr30):
     name="Travel & vacations" current=0 previous=-628 change_amount=628
       change_percent=-100 direction="down"
   change_amount says the group swung UP $628 (correct: no more cruise
   refunds landing, so relative to March's $628 net credit the position
   moved up by exactly that much) while change_percent and direction say
   "down 100%" -- directly self-contradictory within the same struct, on
   the household's own real ledger, one calendar month after the exact
   cruise-refund cluster the spec's own ruling narrative is built around.
   Synthetic isolation (single-category fixture, no other confounds):
   previous=-500 (prior period net refund), current=200 (real $200 spend)
   -> change_amount=700 (correct: up) but change_percent=-140,
   direction="down" (backwards).

This is the same defect class CB3-E exists to prevent (a signed-base
percent-change dividing by the base's own sign instead of |base|) -- the
worker applied that exact lesson to metrics.PercentChange (CB3-E, correctly
pinned) but did not check trends.go's own separate, duplicate percent-change
formula 250 lines away in the SAME FILE they were actively editing for
CB3-D, despite the manifest's "Downstream trace notes" section covering
monthProjection, burnRateChange's guard, verdict.go's BurnRateChange<0
classification, and template rendering -- but not this. It surfaces on the
`get_trends` MCP tool's `major_expense_trends.change_percent`/`direction`
fields and any UI consumer of the same CategoryTrend struct (the get_trends
docstring amendment CB3-b explicitly documents change_percent/direction as
"SIGNED... negative means spending FELL... do not read either as a
magnitude" -- this bug produces exactly a case where that stated contract
is violated by the tool's own output, not just misread by a user).

Commands (reproducible against scratchpad/cb3-run, no repo binary executed):
  cd scratchpad/cb3-run
  # (temporary probe, removed after use -- see process note above)
  go test -run TestCB3NegPrevCheck -v ./internal/services/insights/
    -> NEGPREV name="Airline" current=200 previous=-500 change_amount=700
       change_percent=-140 direction="down"
  go test -run TestCB3RealDataProbeInsights -v ./internal/services/insights/
    -> PROBEI aprtrend name="Travel & vacations" current=0 previous=-628
       change_amount=628 change_percent=-100 direction="down"

